WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.297
In the best-selling books,

00:00:01.297 --> 00:00:04.570
"The Better Angels of our
Nature" and "Enlightenment Now,"

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:06.380
Harvard linguist Steven Pinker made

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.620
the surprisingly controversial
case that humanity

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.120
has been getting richer and less violent

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.870
over the past two centuries.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:15.660
In "Rationality: What It Is

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.090
Why It Seems Scarce and Why It Matters,"

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:21.240
he argues that our ability to
reason and think critically

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.120
is central to human flourishing

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.180
and undergirds our phenomenal
material and moral progress

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.420
since the enlightenment.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:31.130
Pinker explains how
important cognitive defects

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:35.120
such as the sunk-cost
fallacy and myside bias

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.960
cloud our thinking and contribute

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.510
to intensely polarized
tribalistic worldviews

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.870
that result in the Trumpian
rights embrace of Q Anon

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.490
and what he calls
universities' suffocating

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:48.280
left-wing monoculture.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.490
Not afraid to shy away from controversy,

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.410
he insists that public policy
should be largely driven

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:57.200
by facts, not emotion, even
in heart-wrenching cases,

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.480
such as the police
killing of George Floyd.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:03.350
If the goal is to save the
largest number of black lives

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:06.670
or anyone's lives who
are killed by police,

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:08.230
then basing it on a viral video

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:09.830
is probably not the way to go.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:12.490
Reason talked with
Pinker about rationality,

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:16.230
social progress, and why
despite so much negativity,

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.210
fear, and anger in the world,

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.130
he's optimistic about the future.

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.450
Steven Pinker, thanks
for talking to Reason.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.130
Thanks Nick.
So let's start.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:27.350
What's elevator pitch for "Rationality:

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:30.260
What It Is Why It Seems
Scarce and Why It Matters?"

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.520
Rationality is an introduction to

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.600
some of the major tools of reasoning

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.540
that don't necessarily
come naturally to us.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.490
Logic, probability theory,
Bayesian reasoning,

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.463
correlation and causation,
rational choice theory,

00:01:43.463 --> 00:01:44.590
game theory.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:48.620
It's a exploration of
whether and to what extent

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:51.600
your typical human is rational, that is,

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.330
how well do we measure up
against these benchmarks

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:55.740
of reasoning.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.720
It's a explanation as to
why there seems to be such

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.530
a rampant insanity in the world.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:06.460
Belief in paranormal
phenomena, medical quackery,

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:07.500
conspiracy theories.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.800
At one point, you refer to it
as a pandemic of poppycock.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:13.090
Yes.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.780
Pandemic of poppycock.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:19.680
And then I think on the
somewhat paradoxical task

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.340
of making the case for reason.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:25.610
Paradoxical, because unless
you've already endorsed reason

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.780
as the way that we should believe things,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.210
then there are no tools
with which to defend reason.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:32.110
But I do my best.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:33.570
- All right. Let's start with that.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:37.520
Early on you define reason
or rationality rather

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.580
as the use of knowledge to attain goals.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:45.580
And you talk about a tribe in Africa

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:48.310
that people I think, and certainly,

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:49.500
you know, maybe 50 years ago,

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:50.487
or a hundred years ago would say,

00:02:50.487 --> 00:02:53.020
"Well, these people are
primitive. They're not rational."

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:56.380
But you kind of show how
they employ rationality.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:57.710
Could you talk a little bit about that

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.900
and kind of fill in the
definition of rationality

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:01.733
that you're using?

00:03:01.733 --> 00:03:06.733
Yeah, I wanted to push back
against the common trope

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:09.650
often appealing to
evolutionary psychology.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.810
A field that I'm quite sympathetic to.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.240
That, well, what do you expect if he wins?

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:14.710
Of course, we're irrational.

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:17.820
We descended from a
caveman on the savannah

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.320
who just chucks spears at animals.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.483
And had to run up a
tree if they saw a lion.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.487
We're just nothing but a
bundle of quick reflexes.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:31.770
But if you actually look
at the lifestyle of people,

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:36.070
the peoples who survive in
a hunter-gatherer existence

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:37.850
in the Kalahari desert.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.890
The case of the San who I
featured in the first chapter,

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.430
they are highly cerebral.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:46.800
They've got to, they survived
by persistence hunting,

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.550
which is a uniquely human specialty.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:52.810
It takes advantage of the
fact that we are naked.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:54.580
We have no fur, we sweat.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.670
So one thing that we can do in the desert

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.140
that other animals can't is run marathons.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.200
We dump heat.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.170
We don't get heat stroke.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:05.280
Furry animals do, they're faster than us,

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.350
but if you pursue them long enough,

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:10.060
they will keel over.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:14.079
So the trick though is tracking
them because they're fast.

00:04:14.079 --> 00:04:16.790
They see a human, they're out of sight,

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:18.200
but they leave behind tracks.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:22.330
And the San reason from
the tracks to what species

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:24.570
of animal was, what its sex is,

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.410
what its condition is,
where it's likely to be.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:31.100
And they do it even though
lots of tracks look alike,

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.802
the tracks get eroded by wind and rain,

00:04:33.802 --> 00:04:37.450
and they engage in argumentation.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:41.440
They doubt their first instincts.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:42.880
They don't go with their gut.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.730
A young upstart can challenge an elder.

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:51.600
So they don't fall for the
fallacy of argument by authority.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.660
They distinguish
correlation from causation.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.370
They teach Bayesian reasoning.

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:57.470
They use a lot of the tricks of reasoning,

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:02.719
which means that we can't just
excuse our own irrationality.

